WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.880
﻿SIERRA: Hey everyone, a quick note before&nbsp;
we start the show. So some of our colleagues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.740
produced this amazing online presentation&nbsp;
about the international refugee crisis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:15.300
and it’s been nominated for a Webby this&nbsp;
year. I encourage you to check it out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.160
if you like what you see, please vote&nbsp;
for us. Voting closes on May 7th. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:26.520
go to Vote.WebbyAwards.com and search for&nbsp;
it by its title “No Refugee.” And thanks!

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:39.360
Ah, social media. Love it or hate it, we use&nbsp;
it for almost everything. Staying in touch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.440
buying things we need, buying things&nbsp;
we don’t need, following the news,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.420
even checking in during a disaster.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:51.060
But entrusting these platforms with&nbsp;
our information comes at a cost. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:55.200
boundaries of our privacy have faded, and&nbsp;
more and more we find ourselves asking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.480
who’s in charge? Is it legislators?&nbsp;
Is it the platforms themselves? In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.760
U.S. the answers to those questions are unclear.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:08.760
But in India, a potentially decisive moment&nbsp;
in digital freedom is going down right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:13.740
India’s ruling party has put forward&nbsp;
new rules that would allow it to trace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:19.140
and censor private communication. Standing&nbsp;
in its way is WhatsApp, an American-made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.980
encrypted messaging platform that&nbsp;
is used by hundreds of millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:27.060
Indians. And the outcome could have&nbsp;
ripple effects across the globe.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:31.620
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and&nbsp;
this is Why It Matters. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:36.720
India’s government versus WhatsApp, and the&nbsp;
looming threat of digital authoritarianism.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:41.760
GOEL:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:47.460
India is the world's largest&nbsp;
democracy. If you can imagine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:52.080
900 million people voted in the last&nbsp;
national election last year. Out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.420
of a population of 1.3 billion people,&nbsp;
it's just a massive exercised election.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:02.400
MODY: Yeah, it's huge, it's populous, and&nbsp;
that's what also makes it really fun and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.300
exciting to be on the ground. You certainly feel&nbsp;
the energy, especially in a city like Mumbai.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:12.900
GOEL: Politicians routinely insult each other on&nbsp;
the campaign trail, criminals of every flavor run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:19.740
for office and win, so do Bollywood movie&nbsp;
stars, cricket stars. It just never ends.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:23.820
ARUN: India has always been a fairly&nbsp;
chatty democracy. We talk a lot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.380
we argue a lot, that's our culture.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.440
SIERRA: Okay so help me understand why WhatsApp is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.560
such a big deal in India? How many&nbsp;
people are actually using it there?

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.240
ARUN: Roughly 400 million.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.320
SIERRA: That's a lot of people.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:38.760
ARUN: It is indeed.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:43.500
SIERRA: This is Chinmayi Arun, she is&nbsp;
a resident fellow at Yale University,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:48.720
and the founder of a research center at&nbsp;
National Law University in Delhi. She’s also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.240
a leading legal expert on the intersection&nbsp;
between freedom of speech and technology.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:58.860
SIERRA: And so why WhatsApp? Why not,&nbsp;
you know, Instagram, or Snapchat?

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:04.260
ARUN: The elite platforms are used by everyone,&nbsp;
but WhatsApp is the one that really appeals to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:09.420
people. If you have a phone that's not too fancy,&nbsp;
if you don't speak English, you don't read, or you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:14.520
don't have access to an expensive data connection,&nbsp;
you can still use WhatsApp. So it started with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:18.660
"Hey, you don't have to spend one rupee or two&nbsp;
rupees per text, you just save them up and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:23.640
they send whenever you're in Wi-Fi." And so a lot&nbsp;
of people with not a lot of money, which is many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:28.080
many Indians, decided that this works for them.&nbsp;
And then WhatsApp had these multimedia features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.520
which people started using and started liking.&nbsp;
And I have mixed feelings about this because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:36.840
the good thing is that I get to talk to my&nbsp;
grandma, and she finally has forgiven me for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:42.060
moving across the Atlantic, she can look at my&nbsp;
home and say things about my plants or whatever.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:42.540
SIERRA: Of course.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.900
ARUN: But if you're sitting in an Indian&nbsp;
airport and watching the number of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:50.580
who do video calls, they sort of subject&nbsp;
you to their conversations. Mixed feelings.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:58.980
GOEL: WhatsApp is really a messaging platform,&nbsp;
and that can be one-to-one messages like me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:04.860
sending a message to you, but it's also&nbsp;
very commonly used in India for groups.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:13.980
I am Vindu Goel, and I am a technology reporter&nbsp;
for The New York Times based in Mumbai, India.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:18.000
SIERRA: Vindu also covers&nbsp;
Indian economics and culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.660
and has written extensively about&nbsp;
free speech and misinformation under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:25.700
India’s ruling party. We called him&nbsp;
at his home in Mumbai on WhatsApp.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:32.880
GOEL: WhatsApp has become so embedded in&nbsp;
life in India that people use it in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.440
business transactions, so you can order&nbsp;
groceries from your corner grocery store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:44.220
over WhatsApp. I buy an airline ticket from&nbsp;
MakeMyTrip, which is one of the big online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:49.800
travel agencies. They send me a confirmation&nbsp;
message on WhatsApp with my E-ticket details.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:56.520
SIERRA: For those of us based outside of India,&nbsp;
handling a basic transaction may require a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:01.380
few different platforms. You find out about a&nbsp;
concert on Instagram, RSVP for it on Facebook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:06.360
maybe share the notification on Twitter, buy&nbsp;
your ticket on Ticketmaster, and receive the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:11.760
confirmation on Gmail. But for Indians, WhatsApp&nbsp;
is often the one-stop-shop for everything.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.200
In other words, an application&nbsp;
from Silicon Valley has become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:21.540
basic infrastructure for the second-most&nbsp;
populous nation on Earth. That’s a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:25.620
big deal, and it helps illuminate why the&nbsp;
government is pushing for greater control.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:35.760
GOEL: WhatsApp was founded in 2009 by two former&nbsp;
employees of Yahoo, it’s an American company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:41.460
it was founded in Silicon Valley, and they&nbsp;
basically built a very simple messaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:45.900
service. It became very popular, and it&nbsp;
caught the attention of Mark Zuckerberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:56.040
the chief executive and founder of Facebook.&nbsp;
Zuckerberg decided in 2014 to buy WhatsApp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:02.805
and pay the still astonishing price of&nbsp;
$22 billion for this messaging company.

00:06:02.805 --> 00:06:03.720
SIERRA: Yikes. That's a lot.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:05.460
GOEL: It's a lot.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:09.480
LA Times: Facebook is making&nbsp;
a mobile push with its deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:11.820
to buy real-time messaging service, WhatsApp.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:16.250
CNN: It's still an incredible price to pay&nbsp;
for a company that’s just a few years old.

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:20.640
ABC News: It also makes tiny WhatsApp&nbsp;
more valuable than some of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:26.100
established companies in the country, including&nbsp;
American Airlines, Marriott Hotels, and Xerox.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:32.280
MODY: And it came at a crucial moment for&nbsp;
Facebook when it really was trying to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:38.400
ways to diversify its revenue base, and&nbsp;
also get in touch with more mobile users.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:46.320
I'm Seema Mody, global markets correspondent&nbsp;
with CNBC Business News. And suddenly came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:52.140
this messaging platform that was not only gaining&nbsp;
prominence here in the U.S., but around the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:57.840
In fact, I believe that the average daily user&nbsp;
rate on WhatsApp was much higher than Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.640
Messenger. Facebook saw that and said, “this&nbsp;
is such a strategic bet for us, let's acquire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:07.680
it and find a way to really incorporate&nbsp;
it into our user base and our platform.”

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.360
SIERRA: But WhatsApp is free, right?

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.560
MODY: WhatsApp is free and I think&nbsp;
this is still a developing story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:18.000
to see how Facebook is really trying to&nbsp;
incorporate WhatsApp into its business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:22.740
and will you one day see ads on WhatsApp?&nbsp;
That's certainly been one of the big questions.

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:29.400
GOEL: Zuckerberg was prescient in that this&nbsp;
technology of a very simple messaging app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:35.700
was going to become very popular. Today, WhatsApp&nbsp;
has more than two billion users around the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:44.820
It is by far the most popular messaging app in the&nbsp;
world, and one of its biggest claims to fame is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:51.960
emphasizes privacy. All messages on the service&nbsp;
use something called end-to-end encryption.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:58.560
SIERRA: Okay so, in most cases when you send an&nbsp;
email, or a text message, it gets encrypted. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:04.260
means that the information inside is locked up in&nbsp;
a code, so that outsiders can’t read it. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:09.960
the service providers that pass your message along&nbsp;
can read it - whether that’s Apple, or Gmail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:15.480
or Facebook, whoever. They all have the keys to&nbsp;
that code. And that makes your message vulnerable.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:21.060
Enter end-to-end encryption. With this&nbsp;
technology, none of the middlemen have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:24.840
the keys. Only you and the person you&nbsp;
are sending it to can break the code.

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:31.080
GOEL: WhatsApp can't read it, even if the&nbsp;
government came knocking at the door of WhatsApp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:36.360
WhatsApp would have nothing really to give&nbsp;
them, and so your information is private.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:41.640
SIERRA: And that makes it unique to other&nbsp;
services, messaging services, like, whether you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:47.060
sending a text message, or even Facebook Messenger&nbsp;
where that information does live somewhere.

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:52.980
GOEL: Yeah. Most other messaging services&nbsp;
in the world are not end-to-end encrypted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:56.940
and certainly none of the really popular&nbsp;
ones. But WhatsApp has made it really easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.940 --> 00:08:59.940
you don't have to think about&nbsp;
encryption. It just is encrypted.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:04.260
SIERRA: Because so much of India’s&nbsp;
communication happens on WhatsApp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:07.920
end-to-end encryption has made it&nbsp;
very difficult for the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.880
to investigate messages, in&nbsp;
the name of national security.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:16.980
ARUN: People are not making phone calls anymore.&nbsp;
They're not even walking over to their neighbor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:21.600
and saying things, they're just texting them. And&nbsp;
where there is speech, there is harmful speech.

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:28.200
SIERRA: It’s not news that social media can&nbsp;
bring out the worst in people. The platforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:33.600
we use every day are teeming with sexism,&nbsp;
racism, misinformation, and violent ideologies.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:36.660
It’s the same in India, where social media has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:39.180
amplified problems that are a&nbsp;
lot older than the Internet.

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:43.800
PBS NewsHour: Rumors and lies spread&nbsp;
like wildfire across the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:46.488
including across chat applications like WhatsApp.

00:09:46.488 --> 00:09:50.400
Al Jazeera: There are many first&nbsp;
time users in India who think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:53.460
whatever comes in their WhatsApp inbox, is true.

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:58.980
ARUN: The one that's really made the headlines&nbsp;
is that there was a lot of vicious hate speech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:03.720
circulating on WhatsApp. So for example, the&nbsp;
Muslim community is under quite a lot of pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:08.340
in India right now and it's really sad. One of&nbsp;
the ways in which they're discriminated against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:15.600
is that some upper-caste Hindus, don't eat beef.&nbsp;
And so the rumor circulating on WhatsApp will say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:21.840
things like, "X has beef in his fridge." Or, "Y&nbsp;
is his transporting a cow carcass." And since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:28.080
it's already been sold to people as a threat to&nbsp;
their religion, when a rumor like that reaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:31.680
people that already feel threatened, and feel&nbsp;
like these people are out to get all the Hindus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:35.202
and they're trying to destroy our religion&nbsp;
by eating beef, and lynch mobs attack them.

00:10:35.202 --> 00:10:35.214
SIERRA: Wow.

00:10:35.214 --> 00:10:38.340
ARUN: And they're popularly&nbsp;
called the WhatsApp lynchings.

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:50.400
GOEL: In early 2018, there was a wave of false&nbsp;
messages on WhatsApp about child kidnappers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:57.600
prowling various parts of India trying to steal&nbsp;
people's children, and this panicked a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:06.360
people and mobs attacked strangers in various&nbsp;
parts of India and killed them. Beat them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:13.620
death, tied them up, hung them, all kinds&nbsp;
of terrible things. More than 20 people died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:21.420
in just a span of a few months because of these&nbsp;
rumors. And after these rumors started appearing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:28.380
the central government, went after WhatsApp and&nbsp;
said, "You need to find a way to trace these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:35.760
messages and stop these messages." And this set&nbsp;
up a feud with WhatsApp that has still not been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:39.480
resolved. The government says it doesn't want&nbsp;
to read your messages, the government says they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:43.920
don't want to spy on the content of messages.&nbsp;
They're not asking WhatsApp to break the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:48.360
encryption of messages and just show them what's&nbsp;
in the messages, but what they are saying is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:54.120
"You need to be able to trace back the pathway of&nbsp;
a message, and you have to find a way to do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:59.640
because if some message goes viral, we want&nbsp;
to find out who sent it." And we'll see what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:04.380
happens. I mean, WhatsApp has said that to do&nbsp;
that would require significant changes to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:07.860
service and they haven't said whether they'd be&nbsp;
willing to make such changes to their service.

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:11.820
SIERRA: And that brings us back to&nbsp;
the rule changes that the Indian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:15.000
government is proposing. The&nbsp;
draft hasn’t been finalized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:20.820
but the current language would require platforms&nbsp;
such as Facebook, Instagram, WhatsApp, TikTok,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:26.700
and others to remove unlawful posts, and trace&nbsp;
their origins within 72 hours of a request.

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:29.880
This sounds pretty reasonable. If misinformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:33.480
on a private social media platform&nbsp;
is leading to real-life violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:38.340
the need to have an authority step in and&nbsp;
offer regulation seems like a rational impulse.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:43.620
Still, context matters, and before&nbsp;
we go any further, it’s probably a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:48.720
good idea to learn a bit more about India’s&nbsp;
ruling party, popularly known as the BJP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:53.040
which some critics have described as&nbsp;
authoritarian and ethno-nationalist.

00:12:55.340 --> 00:13:00.360
SIERRA: Can you tell me a little bit&nbsp;
just about the ruling party in India,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:02.640
and how they interact with social media?

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:08.640
ARUN: So, the ruling party in India was known&nbsp;
for their use of social media, modeled on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:13.620
Obama campaign, by the way. And so there's a part&nbsp;
of it in which they've been much lauded for taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:18.300
to the social media and sort of modernizing&nbsp;
Indian politics that way. It's also known for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:23.100
a huge rise in the number of Internet shutdowns.&nbsp;
So it's interesting because they take pride in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:28.980
being accessible to people on social media, but&nbsp;
at the same time, are quite controlling of it.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:30.720
SIERRA: It sounds like they&nbsp;
know how powerful it is.

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:31.920
ARUN: Yes, it does.

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:36.060
SIERRA: So, what do you mean by Internet&nbsp;
shutdowns? Like, they're just completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:39.720
shutting down access to everybody in a&nbsp;
certain place for a certain amount of time?

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:44.940
ARUN: Yes, what I mean by an Internet shutdown&nbsp;
is, a shutdown of all Internet services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:49.140
but typically also telephone services, so&nbsp;
basically it's a full communications shutdown.

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:53.340
SIERRA: That's crazy, and scary. And how&nbsp;
do people react to something like that?

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:59.340
ARUN: Fury, mostly. My colleagues and&nbsp;
I have been tracking this from when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:03.060
it started in Kashmir years ago.&nbsp;
For the Kashmiris, it's terrible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:06.660
because they're stuck completely cut off from&nbsp;
the rest of India and from the rest of the world.

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:12.540
SIERRA: The story of Kashmir could take up an&nbsp;
episode on its own. We don’t want to oversimplify,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:17.580
so check our show notes for the full story.&nbsp;
Kashmir is a Muslim-majority region between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:21.540
India and Pakistan that the two nuclear&nbsp;
states have been fighting over since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:27.600
1947. Tensions have led to war four times&nbsp;
already. In order to tamp down dissent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:33.660
the BJP shut down the Internet in Kashmir for&nbsp;
more than 200 days. This is just one example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:37.440
of how far they’re willing to go in order&nbsp;
to control the flow of information online.

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:46.380
GOEL: The BJP is very good at winning&nbsp;
elections, and one of the skills that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:55.560
it’s really mastered is how to use social media to&nbsp;
disseminate misinformation. They have factories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.560 --> 00:15:02.520
people who are essentially creating content&nbsp;
to put out every day. Some of it's true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:08.580
some of it's false, some of it's half&nbsp;
true. There's a certain irony about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.580 --> 00:15:14.880
BJP-led government seeking help from WhatsApp&nbsp;
to crack down on misinformation when, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:20.600
the very same government is the source of much&nbsp;
of the misinformation that moves on WhatsApp.

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:24.480
SIERRA: So, you don't buy the&nbsp;
argument that the only reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:28.500
the government wants traceability is&nbsp;
to stamp out dangerous disinformation?

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:35.220
GOEL: No. The Indian government has used&nbsp;
surveillance against dissidents in Kashmir.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:42.120
People widely believe that cell phones&nbsp;
are tapped, that SMS messages are being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:48.000
read by the government. There's a huge sort of&nbsp;
black budget for various kinds of surveillance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:51.180
operations that the government does, and&nbsp;
they do it in the name of national security,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:54.720
they say they're finding terrorists, much like&nbsp;
the United States government that does a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:58.800
surveillance in the name of national security. And&nbsp;
the laws are quite broad in India. It essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:04.080
gives the government the right to do surveillance&nbsp;
for pretty much any reason it pleases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:11.420
and so no one really trusts that it would&nbsp;
only be used for good and noble purposes.

00:16:11.420 --> 00:16:16.560
SIERRA: Watchdogs in India have been&nbsp;
concerned about the constitutional right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:21.600
to privacy for a long time. Some critics&nbsp;
see the latest push by the BJP as part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:26.640
a larger consolidation of power and a shift&nbsp;
towards a Hindu nationalist agenda. They’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:30.420
concerned that new surveillance tools&nbsp;
will be used in discriminatory ways.

00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:36.180
GOEL: The request that WhatsApp provide a&nbsp;
way to trace messages as part of a broader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:40.260
set of rules that the Indian government&nbsp;
wants to impose on internet companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.260 --> 00:16:47.760
these rules would also require companies to more&nbsp;
closely monitor for certain kinds of content,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:54.780
would give the government power to require the&nbsp;
removal of content that it considered threatening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.780 --> 00:17:01.920
to the state or public order, a variety of rather&nbsp;
open-ended justifications. And so the risk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:06.180
the concern that a lot of people have is that&nbsp;
essentially this will give the government the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.180 --> 00:17:11.280
power to censor anything at once and&nbsp;
force the internet companies to comply.

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:18.900
SIERRA: So, in response, has WhatsApp&nbsp;
done any other things? Have they made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:22.860
other moves to sort of placate&nbsp;
this request and say, look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.860 --> 00:17:27.480
we're taking care of this misinformation&nbsp;
that is truly leading to people dying?

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:34.260
GOEL: Yes. So, one step they took&nbsp;
was to limit the number of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:37.560
or groups you could forward a message to, to five.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:43.680
People would get some exciting message or people&nbsp;
would get some threatening message and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:47.100
"I need to let everybody I know, know about this,"&nbsp;
without really thinking about whether it's true or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:50.940
not, or knowing, and then all of a sudden this&nbsp;
forward shows up and you don't really know where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:56.460
it came from, and it goes viral. And so the idea&nbsp;
was to slow the speed, slow the dissemination. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.460 --> 00:18:00.480
they tested that in India and they found it worked&nbsp;
so well that they've now rolled it out globally.

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:06.180
SIERRA: In fact, since we interviewed Vindu,&nbsp;
WhatsApp has made further restrictions. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:09.600
a user receives a message that has&nbsp;
already been forwarded five times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:14.280
they can now only send it to a single&nbsp;
person or group. The changes are aimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:17.640
at stemming the flow of viral&nbsp;
misinformation about COVID-19.

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:25.200
SIERRA: I mean, it sounds like WhatsApp is making&nbsp;
efforts to address these issues just without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:29.760
breaking encryption. That seems to be the main&nbsp;
thing that they're refusing to do at this point.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:33.540
ARUN: So far. I believe that they're&nbsp;
now under pressure to break encryption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:37.080
also. And that's why I said they're in a&nbsp;
hard place, because if it comes to that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:42.180
if it comes to either leave the country&nbsp;
or break encryption, the question is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:48.000
are they going to give up all the big money, the&nbsp;
big market, or are they going to break encryption?

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:50.400
SIERRA: So, what will happen if WhatsApp refuses?

00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:55.020
ARUN: So I think if you asked WhatsApp&nbsp;
the answer to this question, they'll say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:59.880
"Well, another player will move in." I don't&nbsp;
think that it'll be as easy or as smooth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:05.580
because WhatsApp offers functionality that not all&nbsp;
platforms do. And the second thing we don't know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:11.220
because WhatsApp has never tried it, is&nbsp;
that how far will the public be okay with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:15.900
this? So if you tell the Indian public that,&nbsp;
basically, the Indian government said enable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:21.240
surveillance or leave, and therefore none of&nbsp;
you have WhatsApp anymore in this enormous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:26.820
country with fairly rebellious people. It&nbsp;
would be interesting to see how that lands.

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:31.560
SIERRA: So, what is at stake for&nbsp;
Indians as this all plays out?

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:36.420
ARUN: One is that it kills all the wonderful&nbsp;
things that the Internet brought to India.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:43.680
All the young women that learned to rebel quietly.&nbsp;
The organizing, people who learned because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:48.780
were a part of WhatsApp groups that helped educate&nbsp;
them, as I told you in the beginning, human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:54.360
communication moved to WhatsApp. And the thing&nbsp;
is that India has always been a fairly chatty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.360 --> 00:19:59.280
democracy. We talk a lot, we argue a lot, that's&nbsp;
our culture. Amartya Sen's written about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:04.560
and he said, "We didn't need free speech to create&nbsp;
public discourse in India. It was always a thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:09.960
We were always arguing and talking." And we've&nbsp;
never had a state that's been able to monitor that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:16.320
end-to-end. Now that our communication's moved&nbsp;
online, and one private company holds it all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:19.500
if that private company chooses to share&nbsp;
that communication with the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:24.660
for the first time possibly in Indian&nbsp;
history, the Indian government will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:29.880
able to conduct mass surveillance in India.&nbsp;
And so the question is, what will that do to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:34.640
both the Indian formal democracy, but&nbsp;
also the democratic spirit of India.

00:20:34.640 --> 00:20:41.880
GOEL: If there is censorship on a wide scale&nbsp;
you're really going to see a weakening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:48.600
perhaps crushing over time, of that democratic&nbsp;
spirit. India will move more in the direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:53.760
of China where the government decides&nbsp;
which information gets out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:58.380
and every once in a while somebody finds a&nbsp;
way around the censors and posts information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.380 --> 00:21:03.540
that the government doesn't want them to post.&nbsp;
That's the risk with India, is the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.540 --> 00:21:08.840
becomes the source of information and you lose&nbsp;
some of these other sources of information.

00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:16.500
SIERRA: As a reminder, there are 1.3 billion&nbsp;
people living in Indians. 1.3 billion people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:22.560
whose freedoms could be eroded. And the decision&nbsp;
that goes down in India won’t just stay there. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:26.940
could shape the digital freedoms of people&nbsp;
all across the world in the years to come.

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:35.760
GOEL: So, the precedent that this would set, if&nbsp;
WhatsApp added traceability, is India would not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:40.740
be the only country to ask. Other countries would&nbsp;
ask. Once it's there, anybody can ask for it.

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:45.660
ARUN: It could be the beginning of a&nbsp;
movement. And so, Pakistan has introduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.660 --> 00:21:51.060
a new draconian law recently that appears&nbsp;
to be modeled on the Indian one. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:55.320
my Pakistani friends and I joke that our countries&nbsp;
sometimes borrow the worst ideas from each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.980 --> 00:22:02.220
So there's that. There's Kenya, that used&nbsp;
India's digital ID system, and may feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:06.360
like they can do this too, with WhatsApp. I&nbsp;
believe that Israel and Brazil have also had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:12.060
what they might term as “WhatsApp problems.”&nbsp;
And so, you might have a copycat situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:16.920
of many countries' authoritarian rulers feeling&nbsp;
that, "Well, if WhatsApp gives way here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:22.020
it will give way in my country too." So that's&nbsp;
the digital rights problem, that it could spread,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.020 --> 00:22:26.780
and you end up with authoritarian countries around&nbsp;
the world, and that's dangerous for everyone.

00:22:26.780 --> 00:22:33.720
SIERRA: It’s still unclear how and when the new&nbsp;
rules will be implemented, but the twist is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.720 --> 00:22:39.300
a big part of the outcome rests in the hands of&nbsp;
a private American company. WhatsApp could comply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:44.340
with the request, they could attempt to negotiate&nbsp;
and compromise, or they could just refuse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:48.320
and see whether the Indian government&nbsp;
would truly push them out of the country.

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:57.960
MODY: I think when you're that big, you do inherit&nbsp;
some level of responsibility to set the standard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.960 --> 00:23:03.840
and ensure you're not just trying to monetize a&nbsp;
country's populous nation, and instead are coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:08.460
in to try to really work with the country,&nbsp;
to do the right thing. I think as a company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.460 --> 00:23:12.780
you could argue, if they come into a foreign&nbsp;
country, they should be trying to do more good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:18.900
than bad. And it's interesting, if you look at the&nbsp;
CEOs of some of the most powerful tech companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:23.700
in the last two years, the number of trips&nbsp;
individuals like Mark Zuckerberg, Tim Cook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:29.340
Jeff Bezos have taken to India to spend time&nbsp;
with Prime Minister Modi and really try to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.340 --> 00:23:34.500
up their personal relationship with the Indian&nbsp;
leader, I think it just speaks to how much they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:39.260
see India as such a big opportunity, but also to&nbsp;
the big challenges they're facing on the ground.

00:23:39.260 --> 00:23:42.600
SIERRA: But the ultimate&nbsp;
draw is just the market size?

00:23:42.600 --> 00:23:47.580
MODY: I think that's exactly what it is. 1.3&nbsp;
billion people, half of which are under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.580 --> 00:23:53.220
age of 25. These companies see this as&nbsp;
a huge opportunity, especially at a time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.220 --> 00:23:59.280
when China's economy is slowing down, you&nbsp;
look at India as that next big bet in Asia.

00:24:00.020 --> 00:24:07.440
GOEL: I think that all of these companies have&nbsp;
become very powerful. We use their services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:13.320
we trust lots of information to them.&nbsp;
Sometimes they treat it with great respect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:19.740
sometimes they abuse that trust, and people are&nbsp;
distrustful of the tech companies as much as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:26.760
they're also distrustful of the government. Tech&nbsp;
firms need to figure out how to address some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:34.500
these very real concerns raised by governments&nbsp;
without fundamentally altering the nature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:40.260
of the product. I'm not sure it's possible. The&nbsp;
attitude in Silicon Valley for a long time was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:45.900
"We're not going to help. We don't want&nbsp;
to help." Twitter quite famously called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.900 --> 00:24:50.040
itself the free speech wing of the free speech&nbsp;
party. They don't say that very much anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:55.500
because it's complicated, right? So, Twitter&nbsp;
became this cesspool of trolls and attacks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:55.500 --> 00:25:01.800
all kinds of nasty stuff, and they were losing&nbsp;
users and they had a bad reputation among a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:07.020
of people and they realized that they have to&nbsp;
figure out how to draw lines. But where do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.020 --> 00:25:11.400
draw the lines? There's no easy solutions. This&nbsp;
technology has in many ways gone out of control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:17.820
no one can really control it, even the companies&nbsp;
themselves have trouble controlling how it's used.

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:23.400
SIERRA: Right. I mean in an ideal&nbsp;
world if every government was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:27.420
perfect and amazing and benevolent, of&nbsp;
course they should be a part of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:27.420 --> 00:25:31.380
and of course they should be helping&nbsp;
monitor something that is so out of control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.380 --> 00:25:36.840
but you can't trust that everybody has the&nbsp;
best interests at heart and wouldn't take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:36.840 --> 00:25:42.540
advantage of a situation. And it sounds like&nbsp;
there's just no easy answers for these things.

00:25:42.540 --> 00:25:46.620
GOEL: No. I mean, these are democracies,&nbsp;
right? Autocracies are even worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:50.880
but even in a democracy, even in a democracy&nbsp;
like India, a democracy like the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:57.480
there's always an opposition, and whoever's in&nbsp;
power is going to be tempted to use these tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.480 --> 00:26:03.000
to hurt the opposition. It's just the nature&nbsp;
of politics and the nature of temptation.

00:26:03.860 --> 00:26:10.440
SIERRA: Our freedoms are increasingly&nbsp;
expressed and exercised online. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:12.540
by concentrating our freedom of speech online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:17.220
we have also made it more susceptible than&nbsp;
ever before to surveillance and censorship.

00:26:17.220 --> 00:26:19.800
Nobody knows the right way to deal with this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.800 --> 00:26:24.240
and the argument is being hashed out,&nbsp;
in different ways, all over the world.

00:26:25.020 --> 00:26:28.200
Soon we’ll hear what the world’s&nbsp;
biggest democracy has to say.

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:44.040
As we mentioned throughout the episode, there is&nbsp;
a whole lot more to learn about India, WhatsApp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:44.040 --> 00:26:51.240
and everything else that plays into this complex&nbsp;
topic. So visit CFR.org/Whyitmatters and take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.240 --> 00:26:55.200
look at the show notes, we added a bunch of&nbsp;
really great stuff to take a deeper look.&nbsp;

